course: books
lesson: 30
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter II, part 2: The Open Road (Toad's canary-coloured caravan)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: They find Toad in a garden chair with a big map on his knees. He jumps up, shakes their paws, dances round them and says he was just about to send for them — he needs them badly. His house is the «finest house on the whole river», he boasts, «or anywhere else» — and when he sees the Rat nudge the Mole, he goes red, then laughs it off.
explain: The Rat, «with an innocent air», asks about his rowing (he can guess the answer). «Pooh! boating!» says Toad — a silly waste of time; he has found «the real thing». He leads them to the stable-yard, and there stands a brand-new horse-drawn caravan, canary-yellow with green trim and red wheels.
explain: The open road! cries Toad: travel, change, excitement, a new place every day! Inside, everything is tiny and clever — bunks, a fold-up table, a stove, a bird in a cage, food of every kind. And «we» start «this afternoon».
explain: The Rat flatly refuses: he's sticking to his river, and so is the Mole. The loyal Mole agrees — but adds, wistfully, that it sounds rather fun. The Rat sees how much the Mole wants it, and wavers.
explain: Toad cleverly invites them to lunch «to talk it over», and talks so wonderfully about the trip that by the end it's somehow settled. The Rat gives in, because he can't bear to disappoint his friends.
fact: Toad was «resting in a wicker garden-chair, with a pre-occupied expression of face, and a large map spread out on his knees» (pre-occupied = thinking hard about something else).
fact: «Hooray! ... this is splendid!» He said he was just going to send a boat down the river to fetch Ratty. He shook both their paws «never waiting for an introduction to the Mole».
fact: «Finest house on the whole river ... Or anywhere else, for that matter,» he «could not help adding». When the Rat nudged the Mole, Toad «turned very red», then laughed: «It's only my way, you know.»
fact: «O, pooh! boating! ... Silly boyish amusement. I've given that up long ago. Sheer waste of time». He has found «the real thing, the only genuine occupation for a life time», and can «only regret the wasted years that lie behind me»; he even calls the Rat's river «your dull fusty old river».
fact: The caravan was «shining with newness, painted a canary-yellow picked out with green, and red wheels». The book calls it a «gipsy caravan» — an old phrase; today «gipsy» is seen as an unkind word for Gypsy, Roma and Traveller people, so we just say a horse-drawn caravan.
fact: Toad: «The open road, the dusty highway, the heath, the common, the hedgerows, the rolling downs! ... Here to-day, up and off to somewhere else to-morrow!» He says he planned the inside «all myself».
fact: Inside: «Little sleeping bunks—a little table that folded up against the wall—a cooking-stove, lockers, bookshelves, a bird-cage with a bird in it; and pots, pans, jugs and kettles». In the locker: «biscuits, potted lobster, sardines». The Mole climbed in eagerly; the Rat only snorted and stayed outside.
fact: The Rat, chewing a straw: «did I overhear you say something about "we," and "start," and "this afternoon?"»
fact: Toad: «I'm going to make an animal of you, my boy!» The Rat: «I'm not coming, and that's flat. And I am going to stick to my old river, and live in a hole, and boat».
fact: The Mole: «I'll always stick to you, Rat ... All the same, it sounds as if it might have been—well, rather fun, you know!» he added «wistfully» (wistfully = longingly, a bit sadly).
fact: «The Rat saw what was passing in his mind, and wavered. He hated disappointing people, and he was fond of the Mole».
fact: Toad «diplomatically»: «Come along in, and have some lunch ... We needn't decide anything in a hurry. Of course, I don't really care. I only want to give pleasure to you fellows. "Live for others!" That's my motto in life.»
fact: At lunch Toad played «upon the inexperienced Mole as on a harp» — he knew just what to say to excite him; the Rat «allowed his good-nature to over-ride his personal objections».
fact: Old words: «boisterously» = loudly and wildly; «civil» = polite; «amiable» = friendly; «squandered in trivialities» = wasted on unimportant things; «voluble» = talking a lot; «doggedly» = stubbornly; «baccy» = tobacco.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «it's giving main character» = acting like the star of the show; «flex» = show off; «sound» (Liverpool) = fine, OK; «don't have a cow» (old US) = calm down; «bare» (London) = lots of; «loonie» (Canada) = a one-dollar coin.
exercise: What is Toad looking at when they find him? | A large map | A newspaper about motor-cars | His new racing boat | A letter from Badger
exercise: What does Toad do without waiting for an introduction? | Shakes the paws of both of them | Rows them round the creek in his best boat | Asks the Mole's name | Shows them the boat-house
exercise: What was Toad «just going to» do before they came? | Send a boat to fetch the Rat | Paint the caravan a brighter yellow | Go to bed | Visit Badger
exercise: «Finest house on the whole river». What does Toad add? | «Or anywhere else, for that matter» | «Though Badger's is nicer» | «And the cheapest too, as I built it all myself» | Nothing
exercise: What does Toad do when he sees the Rat nudge the Mole? | Turns very red, then laughs | Gets angry and orders them out of Toad Hall at once | Cries | Pretends not to see
exercise: What does Toad call boating now? | «Silly boyish amusement» | «The only thing worth doing in the whole world» | «The poetry of motion» | «Good fun»
exercise: Where does Toad take them to see his new craze? | The stable-yard | The boat-house | The banqueting-hall | The river
exercise: What colours is the caravan? | Canary-yellow, green trim, red wheels | Blue outside and white within | All black, with gold | Red with yellow wheels
exercise: Who does Toad say planned the inside of the caravan? | He did, all himself | A famous builder from Town | The Rat | The old grey horse
exercise: What lives in the caravan in a cage? | A bird | A white mouse | Nothing | A May-fly
exercise: Which food is NOT named in the locker? | Gingerbread | Biscuits | Potted lobster | Sardines
exercise: What does the Rat do while the Mole goes inside? | Snorts and stays outside | Climbs up on the roof of the caravan to look at the view | Goes home | Harnesses the horse
exercise: When does Toad say they will start? | This afternoon | Next spring | Tomorrow at dawn | After the summer
exercise: «I'm going to make an animal of you, my boy!» Who says it to whom? | Toad to the Rat | The Rat to the Mole | The Mole to Toad | Toad to the horse
exercise: What does the Rat say at first? | «I'm not coming, and that's flat» | «What a splendid idea, Toady» | «Only if Badger comes» | «Let me think»
exercise: What does the Mole say «wistfully»? | It might have been «rather fun» | «I'd rather die than go anywhere in a cart» | «Let's go now» | «Whatever Toad says»
exercise: «he added, wistfully». What does «wistfully» mean? | With a sad kind of longing | Loudly, so everyone could hear | Angrily | Quickly
exercise: Why does the Rat start to waver? | He sees how much the Mole wants to go | Toad offers him money and a new boat | He's hungry | He hates the river
exercise: What does Toad suggest «diplomatically»? | Lunch, to talk it over | A race on the river to decide who's right, Toad or the Rat | A vote | A game of cards
exercise: What does Toad say is his motto? | «Live for others!» | «Never give up!» | «Poop-poop!» | «Up tails all!»
exercise: «he proceeded to play upon the inexperienced Mole as on a harp». What does this mean? | He knew just how to excite the Mole | He played music to the Mole on a real harp after lunch | He tickled him | He bullied him
exercise: Why does the Rat finally give in? | He can't bear to disappoint his friends | He suddenly loves caravans | Toad threatens him | He loses a bet
test: Sam, sound (fine) with it: «Toad was dead modest about his house». Put it right. | He boasted it was the finest anywhere | Right: he said it was just an ordinary little house, nothing special at all | He hated it | He had no house
test: Why does the Rat ask about rowing «with an innocent air»? | He's teasing — he knows Toad has given it up | He really thinks Toad is a champion rower and wants to learn from him | He forgot | He wants to borrow a boat
test: Toad says he only wants «to give pleasure to you fellows». What does he really want? | His own way — to go on his trip with them | To make the Rat happy by staying at home on the river with him | To be alone | Lunch
test: «Of course, I don't really care.» Does Toad mean it? | No — he cares a lot and is being crafty | Yes: he truly doesn't mind at all whether they come or stay on the river | He's too sleepy | Yes, he's honest
test: Ava, flexing (showing off): «the Rat begged Toad to let him come.» Put it right. | The Rat refused flatly at first | Right: he begged on his knees in the stable-yard | Toad begged the Mole | The horse begged
test: Why is the Mole so tempted by the caravan? | Adventure is new and thrilling to him | He has always lived in caravans and misses the life on the road | He hates the Rat | He wants the bird
test: What would have happened if the Rat had stayed firm and said no? | The Mole would have stayed loyally with him | The Mole would have gone anyway, sneaking off with Toad that very night | Toad would cry | Nothing
test: The book calls the caravan a «gipsy caravan». How do we see that word today? | As an old word many Roma and Travellers find unkind | As the polite name everyone still uses for Roma people today | A kind of horse | A colour
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Otter and the Rat laughed about Toad. What did the Rat say happens to all Toad's crazes? | He gets tired and starts something fresh | He gets better and better at each one until he is the champion | He sells them | He forgets them at once
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat said: «I've never been there, and I'm never going». Where, and how does this fit his answer to Toad? | The Wide World — he wants to stay at home | Toad Hall — he hates it | The Wild Wood | Town
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say «It's my world, and I don't want any other» about? | The River | Toad Hall | The Wild Wood | His boat, which he had painted blue and white himself
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Mole first get excited about boats? | The Rat's little blue-and-white boat came to fetch him | He read about boats in a book | Toad showed him | He owned one
test: Toad calls it a «dull fusty old river». How would the Rat of chapter I answer? | It's family, food and fun to him | He'd agree that the river is dull and fusty and he is tired of it | He never liked rivers | He'd ask the Otter
test: ★★★ Toad calls boating a «sheer waste of time» and talks about «the wasted years». But in chapter I he was rowing his hardest in a brand-new boat. What does that show? | Each new craze makes him scorn the last one | He never really rowed | He was rowing to sell the boat | He's always been sensible
test: ★★★ Count the things inside the caravan in this list: «Little sleeping bunks—a little table—a cooking-stove, lockers, bookshelves, a bird-cage». How many kinds? | 6 | 5 | 4 | 7
test: ★★★ «Somehow, it soon seemed taken for granted by all three of them that the trip was a settled thing». What does «somehow» suggest? | Nobody really decided — Toad's talk just carried them along | They voted carefully | The Rat chose | The horse chose
exam: Identify the article in which Toad professed to have discovered «the real thing». | A canary-coloured caravan | A wager-boat | A motor-car | A house-boat
exam: Ascertain the position initially adopted by the Rat in respect of the proposed expedition. | He declined, flatly | He consented with enthusiasm | He proposed a postponement until spring | He volunteered to drive
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the motto professed by Toad. | «Live for others!» | «Live for oneself!» | «Waste not, want not» | «Never look back upon the river»
exam: It is given that the Rat ultimately acceded to the expedition. Identify the principal reason for this. | He could not bear to disappoint his two friends | He had long desired to travel the open road | He was compelled by law | He wished to sell his boat
